AB  - This study aims to assess time management skills among engineering students at Hanoi University of Science and Technology, using a measurement scale developed based on the Time Management Behavior Scale and the Assessment of Time Management Skills. A quantitative cross-sectional survey was conducted with 335 students from various technical disciplines. Descriptive statistics, including mean, standard deviation, and median, were employed to analyze the data. The results revealed no statistically significant differences in time management skills across age or gender. However, correlation analysis indicated significant differences between students who had received time management training and those who had not. These findings underscore the importance of structured soft skills training in enhancing students’ time management abilities and emphasize the need for diverse program content and instructional formats. Additionally, all Cronbach’s alpha coefficients exceeded 0.8, demonstrating the high internal consistency and contextual relevance of the scale. The findings also suggest that this measurement tool holds potential for broader application in higher education research within the Vietnamese context.
